+//! What this process says about itself: the families it counts, the line about one attempt, and the
+//! budget a queue's failures are measured against.
+//!
+//! `jobq/3.13-queue-authz-attribution` made the engine count five things per queue, and the trail of
+//! `jobq/3.6-transaction-isolation` made every settle leave a line. Neither of them decided what an
+//! operator *reads* those as, and that is what this module is: a name, a shape, and the rule that
+//! turns a number into something that wakes a person up.
+//!
+//! **The families are a contract, and they are written once.** [`signals`] is the whole of what this
+//! process exports, and every name in it is there for a reason a query can act on:
+//!
+//! * **a kind, because a counter and a gauge are read differently.** A counter only ever rises, so
+//!   the question asked of it is "how fast" — and the answer is the difference between two
+//!   readings, which is why a rule has a window. A gauge is a reading: asking how fast a gauge is
+//!   rising is a question about the scrape, not about the queue;
+//! * **a label set, and it is small.** The series a family is cut by is the whole of what a query
+//!   can group by, and every value is a series in every collector (`relay/2.18-delivery-metrics`
+//!   applied this to a delivery). Here the axis is the queue, which is the axis a multi-tenant
+//!   queue is asked about — a job id, a worker name or a payload in a label would be a series per
+//!   job, and no alert can be written against a name that changes;
+//! * **a help line, because a number without one is a number somebody guesses at** at three in the
+//!   morning.
+//!
+//! **The sample is the line the process writes.** [`Sample::emit`] writes one line per queue with a
+//! field per family, and the field names *are* the family names — which is what keeps this table
+//! and whoever queries the log from drifting apart. A queue that has seen nothing is reported at
+//! zero rather than left out: a series that appears only after the first failure is a query that
+//! answers "no data" exactly during the quiet period, which is indistinguishable from a queue
+//! that is down (`relay/2.18`'s rule, and the reason [`samples`] takes the queues the deployment
+//! drains rather than the queues the store happens to know).
+//!
+//! **An attempt's line is the other grain, and it is the one that answers "why".** The sample says
+//! "three of this queue's settles in the last five minutes ended badly"; [`Attempt`] says which
+//! jobs, which attempt, and in the handler's own words what went wrong — the line an operator
+//! reads *after* an alert, and the line that leads to the dead letters of `jobq/3.16-dlq-tooling`.
+//! Its fields are the same on every outcome, because a field that is only sometimes there is a
+//! query that has to handle it; the *level* is what differs, and it is part of the contract for
+//! the same reason.
+//!
+//! **A budget is arithmetic over a window, and a window is two readings.** A counter is cumulative
+//! from the day the queue was created, so "the failure rate" is not a property of a reading — an
+//! old queue and a new one with the same rate read differently, and neither reading is the answer.
+//! [`Budget::burn_between`] is that difference, and the two decisions in it are what a budget of
+//! failures means for *this* queue:
+//!
+//! * **the unit is the attempt that ended, not the job that arrived and not the attempt that
+//!   started.** A queue whose submissions stopped has a failure rate of zero and no work at all;
+//!   the budget is about settles. What is *arriving* is a different question, and what is *stuck*
+//!   is the gauge above;
+//! * **a reclaim is a loss and a retry is not.** An attempt that fails and is handed back is the
+//!   retry machinery doing its job — it costs the job an attempt, and the queue nothing. An attempt
+//!   that ends `reclaimed` is work nobody finished: a worker died holding it, or its lease ran out,
+//!   and no failure counter in this crate sees it. A budget that counted only `failed` reads zero
+//!   through a deployment that is losing every job it claims;
+//! * **a share below the floor says nothing.** One failure in one attempt is a 100% failure rate,
+//!   and it is also a Tuesday: a rule that pages on it pages on every quiet hour and on every queue
+//!   that has just been created. [`Budget::floor`] is the traffic a window needs before its share is
+//!   read, and it is a number in the page rather than a default somebody has to know.
+//!
+//! # What is left to write
+//!
+//! Four bodies, and each of them is one of the decisions above:
+//!
+//! 1. [`signals`] — the families, with the names a rule writes and a help line each;
+//! 2. [`Sample::emit`] — the line the process reports, one field per family;
+//! 3. [`Attempt::emit`] — the line about one attempt: the fields a query needs, and the level;
+//! 4. [`Budget::burn_between`] — what two readings of the counters say about the budget.
+//!
+//! Nothing declares this module yet: wiring it into `crate::service` is the first decision, and
+//! until it is made the spec does not compile.
+
+use crate::engine::{JobId, Queue, QueueUsage, Usage, WorkerId};
+
+/// How many parts a share is divided into: a basis point is a hundredth of a percent.
+///
+/// The same unit the service's settings use for anything expressed as a rate, so that "1%" is
+/// written once rather than as `0.01` in one place and `1` in another.
+pub const BASIS_POINTS: u64 = 10_000;
+
+/// The label every family in this module is cut by.
+const QUEUE: &[&str] = &["queue"];
+
+/// The two shapes a family of numbers has over time.
+///
+/// The difference is not cosmetic: it decides what a query may ask. A counter is cumulative and
+/// its rate is a difference between two readings; a gauge is the value at an instant and its
+/// rate is a question about scrapes.
+#[derive(Debug, Clone, Copy, PartialEq, Eq)]
+pub enum SignalKind {
+    /// Cumulative since the process started, and only ever rising.
+    Counter,
+    /// A reading at an instant, which may go up or down.
+    Gauge,
+}
+
+impl SignalKind {
+    /// The word an exposition and a rule table write.
+    #[must_use]
+    pub fn as_str(self) -> &'static str {
+        match self {
+            SignalKind::Counter => "counter",
+            SignalKind::Gauge => "gauge",
+        }
+    }
+}
+
+/// One number this process exports, and everything a query has to know about it.
+///
+/// Fields are private and read through accessors, like [`crate::engine::Job`]: the table below is
+/// the interface, and a family that gains a field is a change inside this module.
+#[derive(Debug, Clone, Copy, PartialEq, Eq)]
+pub struct Signal {
+    /// The name a query and a rule write.
+    name: &'static str,
+    /// Counter or gauge.
+    kind: SignalKind,
+    /// The labels every series of this family carries.
+    labels: &'static [&'static str],
+    /// One sentence: what this number is.
+    help: &'static str,
+}
+
+impl Signal {
+    /// A family, as the table below writes one.
+    #[must_use]
+    pub const fn new(
+        name: &'static str,
+        kind: SignalKind,
+        labels: &'static [&'static str],
+        help: &'static str,
+    ) -> Self {
+        Self {
+            name,
+            kind,
+            labels,
+            help,
+        }
+    }
+
+    /// The name a query and a rule write.
+    #[must_use]
+    pub fn name(&self) -> &'static str {
+        self.name
+    }
+
+    /// Counter or gauge.
+    #[must_use]
+    pub fn kind(&self) -> SignalKind {
+        self.kind
+    }
+
+    /// The labels every series of this family carries.
+    #[must_use]
+    pub fn labels(&self) -> &'static [&'static str] {
+        self.labels
+    }
+
+    /// One sentence: what this number is.
+    #[must_use]
+    pub fn help(&self) -> &'static str {
+        self.help
+    }
+}
+
+/// Every family this process exports.
+///
+/// Five counters and one gauge, which is exactly what the engine keeps
+/// (`jobq/3.13-queue-authz-attribution`) plus the one number that is *derived* there: the gauges and
+/// the counters are the facts, and [`Usage::running`] is what the facts add up to right now.
+///
+/// One family per settle kind rather than one counter with an `outcome` label, and that is the
+/// shape the sample line can carry: its fields are the family names, so a family whose value is a
+/// label's would be one field holding five numbers. The four codes a query groups by are the four
+/// names, and a rule that reads one of them reads the name it was written for.
+#[must_use]
+pub fn signals() -> &'static [Signal] {
+    todo!(
+        "jobq/3.17-error-budget-alerts: the families — a name, a kind and a label set each, with \
+         a help line that says what the number is"
+    )
+}
+
+/// The family a rule names, or `None` when this process does not export one.
+///
+/// This is the rule a step's rules page is judged by: the leftmost column of a rule is a name from
+/// [`signals`], so a page that wakes somebody up for a number nobody counts cannot be written.
+#[must_use]
+pub fn signal(name: &str) -> Option<&'static Signal> {
+    signals().iter().find(|signal| signal.name == name)
+}
+
+/// One queue's reading of every family, and the line it is written as.
+#[derive(Debug, Clone, PartialEq, Eq)]
+pub struct Sample {
+    /// Which queue this reading is about.
+    queue: Queue,
+    /// What it has done.
+    usage: Usage,
+}
+
+impl Sample {
+    /// One queue's reading.
+    #[must_use]
+    pub fn new(queue: Queue, usage: Usage) -> Self {
+        Self { queue, usage }
+    }
+
+    /// Which queue this reading is about — the value of the label every family carries.
+    #[must_use]
+    pub fn queue(&self) -> &Queue {
+        &self.queue
+    }
+
+    /// Write this reading as the line a collector reads: one field per family, plus the label.
+    ///
+    /// The field list is written out rather than looped over: a subscriber indexes a field by a
+    /// literal name, so the table above and this list are two spellings of one set of names. What
+    /// keeps them one set is the test in this step's spec, which reads the fields off a line a real
+    /// process wrote and compares them with [`signals`].
+    ///
+    /// The message is `signals`, and it is what a query selects on: one line per queue per report,
+    /// which is the sample a rule's window is made of.
+    pub fn emit(&self) {
+        todo!(
+            "jobq/3.17-error-budget-alerts: the sample — one field per family, named the way the \
+             table names it, plus the label every family carries"
+        )
+    }
+}
+
+/// One reading per queue the deployment drains, in the order it drains them.
+///
+/// The queues come from the deployment rather than from the store, and that is the whole of the
+/// zero-line rule: a queue the settings name has a series from the first report on, and what the
+/// store answered for it is folded in when there is an answer. A queue with no reading at all is
+/// [`Usage::default`] — six zeroes — rather than a missing series.
+#[must_use]
+pub fn samples(queues: &[Queue], readings: &[QueueUsage]) -> Vec<Sample> {
+    queues
+        .iter()
+        .map(|queue| {
+            let usage = readings
+                .iter()
+                .find(|reading| reading.queue() == queue)
+                .map_or_else(Usage::default, QueueUsage::usage);
+            Sample::new(queue.clone(), usage)
+        })
+        .collect()
+}
+
+/// How an attempt ended, in the words a query groups by.
+#[derive(Debug, Clone, Copy, PartialEq, Eq)]
+pub enum Outcome {
+    /// The attempt finished its job.
+    Succeeded,
+    /// The attempt reported a failure.
+    Failed,
+}
+
+impl Outcome {
+    /// The word the line carries and a query groups by.
+    #[must_use]
+    pub fn as_str(self) -> &'static str {
+        match self {
+            Outcome::Succeeded => "succeeded",
+            Outcome::Failed => "failed",
+        }
+    }
+}
+
+/// One attempt, as the line about it says it.
+///
+/// A value rather than seven arguments at each call site, and that is the decision the step is
+/// about: the fields a query needs are one shape with one writer, so a line that is missing one of
+/// them is a compile error rather than a query that silently returns nothing.
+#[derive(Debug, Clone, Copy)]
+pub struct Attempt<'a> {
+    /// Which queue the job belongs to.
+    queue: &'a Queue,
+    /// Which job.
+    job: &'a JobId,
+    /// Which attempt of that job, as the row counts it.
+    attempt: u32,
+    /// Which worker made it.
+    worker: &'a WorkerId,
+    /// How it ended.
+    outcome: Outcome,
+    /// What went wrong, in the handler's own words; empty when nothing did.
+    reason: &'a str,
+    /// How long the handler took, in whole milliseconds.
+    took_ms: u64,
+}
+
+impl<'a> Attempt<'a> {
+    /// One attempt of one job.
+    ///
+    /// `reason` is the handler's sentence and is written even when the attempt succeeded: the
+    /// field set is the same on every line, and an empty value is what "no reason" looks like to a
+    /// query. `took_ms` is a number rather than a formatted duration, because a collector can add
+    /// numbers and cannot add `1.2s`.
+    #[must_use]
+    pub fn new(
+        queue: &'a Queue,
+        job: &'a JobId,
+        attempt: u32,
+        worker: &'a WorkerId,
+        outcome: Outcome,
+        reason: &'a str,
+        took_ms: u64,
+    ) -> Self {
+        Self {
+            queue,
+            job,
+            attempt,
+            worker,
+            outcome,
+            reason,
+            took_ms,
+        }
+    }
+
+    /// Write the line.
+    ///
+    /// Two things are decided here rather than at the call site, and both are properties of the
+    /// line rather than of the attempt:
+    ///
+    /// * **the level.** A failure is a warning and a success is information — so "everything at
+    ///   this level, in this window" is a query about endings, which is the query an operator has;
+    /// * **the fields.** Queue, job, attempt, worker, outcome, reason and how long it took: the
+    ///   path from a number on a page to the job behind it, which is the whole reason a queue's
+    ///   lines and a queue's counters are worth having together. What is deliberately *not* here is
+    ///   the payload (`crate::service::handler` explains why the row has it and a line does not).
+    pub fn emit(&self) {
+        todo!(
+            "jobq/3.17-error-budget-alerts: the line about one attempt — the fields a query needs, \
+             the same ones on every outcome, and the level that says which outcome it was"
+        )
+    }
+}
+
+/// The budget a queue's failures are measured against.
+///
+/// Two numbers, and both of them are a deployment's decision rather than this crate's: how much of
+/// the work may end badly ([`Budget::allow_bad`], in basis points), and how much traffic a window
+/// needs before its share is read at all ([`Budget::floor`]).
+#[derive(Debug, Clone, Copy, PartialEq, Eq)]
+pub struct Budget {
+    /// Bad basis points the work may spend.
+    allow_bad: u16,
+    /// Settled attempts a window needs before its share is read.
+    floor: u64,
+}
+
+impl Budget {
+    /// The budget this course's rules page is written for: 1% of the settled attempts in a window
+    /// may end badly, and the share is read once a hundred attempts have settled in it.
+    #[must_use]
+    pub const fn standard() -> Self {
+        Self {
+            allow_bad: 100,
+            floor: 100,
+        }
+    }
+
+    /// A budget of `allow_bad` basis points with a floor of `floor` settled attempts.
+    #[must_use]
+    pub const fn new(allow_bad: u16, floor: u64) -> Self {
+        Self { allow_bad, floor }
+    }
+
+    /// Bad basis points the work may spend.
+    #[must_use]
+    pub fn allow_bad(&self) -> u16 {
+        self.allow_bad
+    }
+
+    /// Settled attempts a window needs before its share is read.
+    #[must_use]
+    pub fn floor(&self) -> u64 {
+        self.floor
+    }
+
+    /// What happened between two readings of one queue, measured against this budget.
+    ///
+    /// The subtraction is the window: a counter is cumulative, so what a rule's `[5m]` selects is
+    /// the difference between the reading now and the reading five minutes ago. `saturating` here is
+    /// arithmetic rather than paranoia — the counters of a store that was restored from a backup
+    /// can read lower than they did, and a report that answered a wrapped number under that would
+    /// be worse than one that answered zero.
+    ///
+    /// # What is counted
+    ///
+    /// `total` is the attempts that *ended* in the window: succeeded, failed, reclaimed. `bad` is
+    /// the ones that did not finish their job — the failures, whether or not the job was retried
+    /// (a retry is the budget being spent on purpose), and the reclaims, which is the half no
+    /// failure counter in this crate sees.
+    #[must_use]
+    pub fn burn_between(&self, earlier: &Usage, later: &Usage) -> Burn {
+        todo!(
+            "jobq/3.17-error-budget-alerts: the window — what each counter gained between two \
+             readings, and which of those gains a budget counts as bad"
+        )
+    }
+}
+
+/// What the settled attempts of one window say about a budget.
+#[derive(Debug, Clone, Copy, PartialEq, Eq)]
+pub struct Burn {
+    /// Attempts that ended without finishing their job.
+    bad: u64,
+    /// Attempts that ended at all.
+    total: u64,
+    /// The budget they were measured against.
+    budget: Budget,
+}
+
+impl Burn {
+    /// A window's numbers, measured against a budget.
+    #[must_use]
+    pub const fn of(bad: u64, total: u64, budget: Budget) -> Self {
+        Self { bad, total, budget }
+    }
+
+    /// Attempts that ended without finishing their job.
+    #[must_use]
+    pub fn bad(&self) -> u64 {
+        self.bad
+    }
+
+    /// Attempts that ended at all.
+    #[must_use]
+    pub fn total(&self) -> u64 {
+        self.total
+    }
+
+    /// The bad attempts this window's traffic allows, rounded down.
+    #[must_use]
+    pub fn allowed(&self) -> u64 {
+        u64::from(self.budget.allow_bad).saturating_mul(self.total) / BASIS_POINTS
+    }
+
+    /// `true` while the window holds too little traffic for its share to mean anything.
+    ///
+    /// The rule the noise decision is made of: a share needs a denominator before it is a claim
+    /// about a queue rather than about one unlucky job.
+    #[must_use]
+    pub fn below_floor(&self) -> bool {
+        self.total < self.budget.floor
+    }
+
+    /// `true` when the window spent more of the budget than it was allowed.
+    #[must_use]
+    pub fn exceeded(&self) -> bool {
+        !self.below_floor() && self.bad > self.allowed()
+    }
+}
